  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/5/55/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_104.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Loki is steamed at the Enchantress and Executioner for failing to defeat Thor, but Odin summons Loki to get his advice, still upset that he cannot control Thor's love for the mortal woman.&nbsp; Loki suggests that Odin travel to Earth in person, so Odin transfers part of his power to Loki to \"rule Asgard till my return.\"\n\nMeanwhile Don Blake changes to Thor, certain that Loki will be stirring up some kind of danger, and we see Odin avoid a mugging.&nbsp; But while this is happening, Loki is enjoying the throne and making plans to ensure Odin can never return.&nbsp; He releases Skagg the Storm Giant, whom Odin had imprisoned.&nbsp; Then he breaks \"the spell which has kept Surtur, the Fire Demon, imprisoned within the depths of the Earth.\"\n\nHowever, Loki has not bargained on Heimdall hearing everything that he is doing (thanks to his sharp ears).&nbsp; Heimdall can't leave his post on Bifrost, but he summons Balder the Brave to alert Odin of the new situation.&nbsp; Balder finds Thor on Earth and informs him of the impending danger.&nbsp; Thor realizes that Odin will go straight to Don Blake's office to seek him out, so the two of them head there, where they find Odin and immediately alert him of the danger to Earth.&nbsp; Odin first protects the people of Earth with a spell, sending them all away \"to a dimension beyond the ken of the human mind,\" thereby giving both Balder and Thor the opportunity to strike first.\n\nSoon, the pair of them, alongside Odin himself, engage the pair of attackers that Loki has freed.&nbsp; Surtur becomes a \"circle of pure flaming energy\" and heads north to melt the ice at the Arctic Circle in order to flood the Earth!&nbsp; Odin directly attacks Skagg, not realizing that Loki is replenishing his strength from Asgard.&nbsp; Odin finally tires after he successfully defeats Skagg.\n\nAs for Surtur, Thor realizes that he has too great a head start -- he'll never be able to reach Surtur before the Fire Demon arrives at the northern polar region.&nbsp; Only the oldest weapon of all -- Odin's sword -- may prevail against him, so he grabs the ancient sword and points it up to the heavens.&nbsp; Suddenly, Surtur is pulled away from Earth, drawn magnetically away to a \"very special asteroid,\" composed of magnetic particles \"in another galaxy!\"&nbsp; He's trapped there \"for ages to come!\"\n\nThe battle is won, and Odin suggests they all return home to Asgard.&nbsp; But Thor declares \"I shall <i>never</i> leave!\" because Earth is his home.&nbsp; Odin knows they've settled nothing after all, but releases the Earth people from their spell, congratulates the valiant effort of his two warriors, and returns home.&nbsp; Once there, he deals with Loki by having him \"serve the <i>trolls</i> until I set you free!\"\n\nOdin ponders Thor's future, worried that it is \"fraught with danger, and his heart is weakened by love!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | AskForAdvice | width=15%] [ASSET: right | LokiOnThrone | width=29%] The story starts off on a shaky premise by having Odin asking Loki for advice, simply because he is \"clever,\" even granting him a portion of his power and instructing him to \"rule Asgard till my return!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] <b><i>Clever?</i></b>  This is all very <i>true</i>, but...\n[BR]\n[BR] ...exactly how long does it take for a father to see that his own son is never up to any good?&nbsp; <i>Surely </i>he must surely know Loki <i>hates</i> Thor and would never provide useful advice when it comes to him.&nbsp; <i>Yes, </i>I realize that parents often have a blind spot when it comes to their children, but this stretches believability, especially since Odin has had <i>centuries</i> to observe his step-son.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=(I find this <i>very</i> hard to swallow...)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nNaturally, the <i>moment</i> Odin is gone, Loki tries to unleash holy hell on Earth, all in an attempt to keep Odin from ever returning.&nbsp; At least he remembers where Surtur was, as the fire demon <i>had</i> last appeared in a <i>Tales of Asgard</i> backup feature (in [LINK:1963:JIM_99_thor0|JIM #99|2027-02-06]), which did indeed end with him being trapped inside the Earth.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=That was all well and good.]\n\nThanks to the sharp ears of Heimdall (which readers learn about in the <i>Tales of Asgard</i> portion of <b>this very issue</b>), Balder is sent to inform Odin of Loki's dastardly scheme, and once done (with Thor joining the mix), [SPAN: severity=info; text=I found it interesting that Odin does not so much as <i>blink</i> upon learning that Loki is responsible for the threat about to strike Earth.]\n\nI had previously marveled at the <i>astonishing</i> ability of the New York City authorities to <i>evacuate the entire city</i> in seemingly no time at all!&nbsp; But Odin does them one better here by [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=evacuating the entire <i>planet</i>!]\n[ASSET: center | EvacuatingThePlanet | width=large]\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=small; text=(You gotta hand it to Stan and Jack here for <i>sheer audacity</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | MeltTheIceCaps | width=10%] Though that advances the story to its midway point, that's pretty much where the plot ends, and the next five pages are filled with little more than Kirby-inspired battle action.\n[BR]\n[BR] Surtur heads off to the Arctic Circle, planning to melt the Earth's ice cap and thereby flood the entire planet, all while his partner-in-crime, Skagg, battles Odin, Thor and Balder.\n\nIt's pretty run-of-the-mill action that ultimately ends with Thor snatching up Odin's sword and trapping Surtur on a \"special asteroid\" in \"another galaxy,\" there to be magnetically trapped \"for ages to come!\"\n[ASSET: center | SurturSentAway | width=large]\n\n[HANG0] The [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved,] which is <i>great</i>, but readers can't help but wonder:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Why was it necessary for the asteroid to be in another galaxy?]\n\nAnd let's not even <i>mention</i> the absurd notion that Surtur actually reached the other galaxy in the time it took for Odin to stand up and have his sword handed back to him by Thor.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=That's lickety-split <i>transportation</i> for you!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Though, as Odin had just transported the Earth's <b>entire population</b> to a pocket dimension... maybe we can overlook this...)]\n\nAll that was left to do was essentially have Odin [SPAN: severity=warning; text=forget why he came to Earth in the first place], return to Asgard, deal with Loki, and then continue his brooding over his favorite son, who staunchly refuses to leave Earth, which he describes as \"my home\" and \"the home of the one I love!\"\n\nWhile we <i>do</i> see what happens to Surtur, we are simply left to assume that Skagg was collected up by Odin on his way back to Asgard and dispensed with once again.\n\n[ASSET: left | LokiToServeTrolls | width=10%] <i>Sure</i>, Loki is ultimately sent off by Odin to \"serve the trolls\" until further notice, but other than that...\n[BR]\n[BR] ...the sound you hear is the massive [SOUNDEFFECT: text=RESET] button being pressed!\n[BR]\n[BR] All is well, another 12 cent toll on the readership has been extracted, and off we go to the next adventure!\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nOn the one hand, you have to [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=seriously admire] the first full-scale battle scene between Asgardians and a couple of their otherworldly opponents, which argues for the issue receiving a generous rating.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=The full-on tilt was eye-opening and audacious -- to say the <i>least!</i>]\n\nHowever, one cannot escape the fact that the issue had very little in terms of plot, and I can only imagine Stan saying, \"Hey Jack, let's do an issue where Odin, Balder and Thor battle a couple of Asgardian enemies that Loki set free on Earth!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Seriously?&nbsp; Was there much more than that?]\n\nPerhaps Stan didn't really want to deal with how Odin would address Thor's love for the mortal Jane Foster, because she hardly appears at all.&nbsp; Despite this, Odin <i>does</i> briefly walk amongst mortals, and Balder gets to show off his form as well (however briefly).&nbsp; Having Heimdall demonstrate the very abilities that the <i>Tales of Asgard</i> featurette describes was also a [SPAN: severity=success; text=nice touch.]\n\nYet in the end, Odin never really has time to deal with the original reason he came, and I can only guess that we, as readers, are supposed to let it slide.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=That's a shame.]\n\nI definitely like the cosmic-style action that seems to come hand-in-hand with the title character, and I very much want to see more it, but I question whether Earth was the right place for it.&nbsp; Battles like these seem more appropriate <i>away</i> from Earth, and having it on our own planet required Odin to essentially wave his hands and whisk everyone off somewhere else, with none of them being the wiser.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Stage the battle somewhere else, and there would have been no need for this.]\n\nFurthermore, the entire premise of the story (Odin going to Earth to have words with Thor) was completely overshadowed by this titanic battle and then brushed under the table (for which the story gets points off).\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Let's face it:&nbsp; this was a mixed bag.]\n[BR] It was <i>definitely readable</i> and had some nice moments, but with a bit more care, it could have been better.",
  "side_note": [
    "This is the first time the classic \"The Mighty THOR\" logo is used.&nbsp; Up until this point, the book has been strictly labeled as <i>Journey Into Mystery</i>, but this month (like <i>Tales of Suspense</i> did <i>last</i> month), the old Atlas-oriented name is de-emphasized to prioritize the new direction... and now-main character.\n\nAlso interesting is the announcement that appears at the bottom of the text-only story (\"Poor Oscar\"):\n[ASSET: center | LastBackupStory | width=large]\n\nThis shows that the magazine is <i>fully</i> leaning into Thor and that there will be no further backup stories (other than the normal <i>Tales of Asgard</i> featurette).",
    "<b>Tales of Asgard:&nbsp; </b>\"Heimdall, Guardian of the Mystic Rainbow Bridge\"&nbsp; (beginning a series of \"biographies\")\n(by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby, inked by Don Heck)\n\nOdin summons Heimdall to compete for the job of sentry for the Rainbow Bridge leading to Asgard, since so many of its enemies attack by crossing it.&nbsp; Heimdall is ultimately chosen from a group of three because of his incredibly keen eyes and ears, which miss <i>nothing</i> and provide more than ample warning of approaching threats."
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}